 a day for the market  ever since month by month they've been  adding barrels in  and what they've said is that they're  hoping to balance supply and demand they  don't want to move too quickly and put  too many barrels onto the market they do  see some potential downside risks  the issue here is that as they've added  more oil onto the market each month the  wedge of available spare capacity has  gotten smaller and smaller and in a  tight market like this with a lot of  concerns about low inventories  you know pretty strong demands  people are worried about low spare  capacity so in addition to the question  of whether or not it would really help  in the market to put barrels on  it's kind of a damned if you do them if  you don't situation you know if they put  more barrels under the market and  diminish their capacity it could  backfire in some ways  so  my read on the meeting this week is that  they needed a little bit more time to  digest what was happening and the scale  of the disruption from russia before  they take action i think another thing  at the back of their minds is that we  could have an iran deal happen at some  point very soon  and that would have a real material  impact on balances looking out for the  rest of this year  so i think there are some benefits wait  see how this unfolds and then maybe come  back and make a decision together at  some point soon  yeah let's put that to alex schindler i  mean ben cahill there was talking about  if there's a possibility of reviving the  2015 nuclear uh iran nuclear deal and  the potential of bringing iran  back into the into the oil market uh  what what effect could that have  well it would have a major effect and  and so what we are  hearing is that we are quite close  to a deal being agreed between  the us and western powers and iran to  reinstate the nuclear deal that former  president donald trump walked away from  and this would be a significant addition  of oil to the market from what we  understand