r exports out of russia in  practical terms we actually see a sharp  reduction of flows of uh both coal and  oil out of western russian ports uh ben  cahill uh bjorn mentioned there uh the  the the impact of uh of wheat uh we on  wheat supply so beyond oil we look at  other commodities like like wheat which  uh as we said russia and ukraine are  large suppliers of what what  implications or what impact could that  have on on the global markets  yeah i think beyond makes a good point  that russia is often thought of as an  exporter of oil and gas but in fact they  export a lot of  metals and agricultural commodities too  and so does ukraine by the way for both  the exporters of wheat  i think it adds to the inflationary  concerns that we see around the world  we've already had rising food prices in  a lot of places  you've got regions like the middle east  that are quite dependent on grant  imports so it's going to add to some of  that  economic pressure  and just to back up something that that  bjorn said i mean i think  when we look at the shortages today  we see an immediate impact with fruit  but some of these other commodity  commodities are raw inputs and kind of  base materials that are used in  manufacturing and  lots of other things so the impacts and  the potential price impact we're not  going to see it necessarily today we'll  continue to unfold over a couple months  all right we're going to have to leave  it there thank you to all three of you  alex chandela bjorn shell drop and ben  cahill thanks very much for being on  inside story  and thank you as always for watching  remember you can see this program again  anytime just go to our website  aljazeera.com and for further discussion  go to our facebook page that's  facebook.com forward slash aj inside  story you can also join the conversation  on twitter our handle there is at aj  inside story from me has some seeker and  the whole team here in doha bye for now  [Music]
 

